  faqHeading: "Property Finder integration: what UAE brokerages ask first",
  faqs: [
    { question: "What is a Property Finder feed and how does it work?", answer: "It is a structured document, or an API conversation, in which your own system tells the portal what you currently have advertised. In the common arrangement your system publishes a file at a fixed HTTPS address and the portal collects it on an agreed interval, so a change appears at the next collection rather than instantly." },
    { question: "How much does Property Finder integration cost to build?", answer: `Integration and internal tooling work starts from ${P.software} and is quoted line by line rather than per listing. What moves the figure is how many source systems are involved, whether your current system can export usable data at all, how many portals one record has to serve, and whether the media library needs rebuilding. Your portal subscription is separate and never passes through us.` },
    { question: "Why did all our listings disappear from the portal at once?", answer: "Almost always full-refresh semantics meeting a broken file. The feed is treated as the complete current truth, so anything missing reads as withdrawn, and a timed-out query or a half-written file can remove hundreds of adverts without raising an error. The fixes are an atomic file swap, a record-count threshold that blocks publishing, and a last-good fallback." },
    { question: "Should the advertising permit number be in the feed?", answer: "It should be a dated record in your database that the feed reads, not a string typed into a description. Store the permit number with its issue and expiry dates, the unit and the agent, then block publication when no live permit covers the advert. Obtaining and renewing permits is your brokerage's legal obligation, and the requirement differs by emirate." },
    { question: "Can you check our permit numbers against the government system?", answer: "No. We are three engineers in India with no UAE licence and no broker registration, and we have no access to any government platform. We can enforce that an advert does not publish without a permit record your own staff have entered and dated, and we can warn you before one lapses. Verification against the authority stays with you." },
    { question: "How do we stop two agents listing the same apartment?", answer: "In your own database, not in the feed. Enforce uniqueness on building plus unit number plus listing type so creating a second record requires a deliberate override with a reason, assign one owning agent per unit with an explicit handover step, and model the unit separately from the advert so one apartment can have a history of several adverts." },
    { question: "Will duplicate listings get our agency penalised?", answer: "Portals detect duplication and act on it, and the action lands on the account rather than the advert. Property Finder publishes its own listing and duplication policies and revises them, so read the current version. We build controls that stop your systems creating duplicates and reporting that shows you when a unit appears twice; we cannot appeal anything on your behalf." },
    { question: "What are the photo and floorplan requirements for a property portal?", answer: "Counts, dimensions, file sizes and rules about watermarks, logos, phone numbers and text overlays all come from the portal's own current guidance, and they change. Take that list from Property Finder in writing. Our part is to hold one master image set per unit in your storage, derive every required size automatically, and reject a failing asset before the feed runs." },
    { question: "How should annual rent and cheque counts be stored?", answer: "As an annual amount plus the cheque options you accept, with the amount for each option, because the yearly figure commonly differs by cheque count. Add deposit, agency fee and explicit flags for what the rent includes, particularly cooling charges. One price field cannot express any of this, so the detail ends up in prose where nobody can filter it." },
    { question: "Can portal enquiries land straight in our CRM?", answer: "Yes, by native integration where one exists, by webhook into your own endpoint, or by email parsing as a temporary last resort. The valuable part is what arrives with it: the portal, the advert reference and the arrival time, plus matching on a normalised phone number so one buyer who enquires three ways stays one contact rather than becoming three." },
    { question: "Should our own website show the same listings as the portal?", answer: "Yes, from the same record, but not with the same description text. The portal page will usually outrank yours, so a word-for-word copy earns nothing. Make the durable pages your building, community and bedroom-count pages, treat unit pages as temporary, and return a gone status or redirect when a unit lets rather than leaving it indexed." },
    { question: "Do you support the feed at night if it breaks?", answer: `No, and we say so before you ask. Three freelance engineers in India cannot honestly staff an overnight rota, so we do not sell a guaranteed response window or a 24/7 desk. Monitoring and fixes sit under upkeep from ${P.care} during our working day. If a stopped feed at midnight is a commercial emergency, keep the pager with local staff.` },
  ],
